docs/backlog.md:203:**D151 — DEVDESK-474: a proven absence of route is Fail; it refines D20 and does not contradict it. Decided (user, 2026-11-15): (1) The `route` stage of Diagnostics (`internal/netcheck/stage_route.go`) classifies each lookup error with `nettrace.IsNoRoute`. If every looked-up address is proven without a route, the verdict is Fail; the results header and the MCP verdict go from UNKNOWN to FAIL in that case. (2) D20 stands for everything else: a lookup that could not answer is Unknown when no address is routed, and when some are routed it is OK with reason `route-undetermined`. Warn `partial-route` counts only the proven addresses. (3) Only the first 8 addresses (`maxRoutedAddresses`) are looked up. (4) The system error text goes in the facts `No route` and `Route undetermined`, never in the summary (Rule 129). (5) Known limits: the proof exists on Linux and WSL2 only; on Windows (`windows.Errno` not recognized) and macOS (error without errno) the result stays `undetermined`; if `go-netroute` stopped wrapping the netlink errno with `%w`, Linux would fall back to `undetermined` silently; `prohibit` routes (`EACCES`) stay `undetermined`. (6) Follow-up: a story to create for Windows and macOS. Documented in `docs/architecture/network.md`, Diagnostics tab.**
